  "body": "Conjecture: parse_content (open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.parse_content) returns the same verdict for the reply texts \"3.14\" and \"{}\". The documented rule says: \"The refusal heuristic decides the verdict only when no object can be recovered; whether a phrase occurred at all is a separate fact, refusal_phrase_in, recorded beside a recovered object (H36: a refusal followed by a form is a refusal and a form).\" For \"3.14\", no object is recovered (a float is not an object, and recover_json_object passes over floats), and no refusal phrase is present, so the rule requires INVALID_JSON. For \"{}\", an object is recovered, so the rule requires JSON_OBJECT. The function, however, first tries loads_strict(content) and returns JSON_OBJECT on any successful strict parse, so it returns JSON_OBJECT for both. I would be wrong if parse_content returns INVALID_JSON for \"3.14\" (for example, if loads_strict rejects non-object JSON values or if recover_json_object is consulted before the verdict is set).",
